<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#syriac">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syriac</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Syriac is a dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> that became the main literary and liturgical language of many Christian communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> from the early centuries CE. Syriac-speaking scholars translated and transmitted Greek philosophy and science. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> note their presence at Gundeshapur, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> describe the translation of Syriac texts into Arabic as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>'s great translation movement.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The House of Wisdom (Bayt al-Hikma), traditionally associated with Harun al-Rashid and reaching its zenith under al-Ma'mun, sponsored the systematic translation of Greek, Persian, Indian and <b>Syriac</b> texts into Arabic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The persecution of the Christians of Najran in 523 was commemorated in <b>Syriac</b> and Greek texts such as the Book of the Himyarites and the Martyrdom of Arethas, and Muslim tradition has often linked it with the 'people of the ditch' mentioned in the Qur'an.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of the population consisted of Muslim peasants and Eastern Christians, including Greek Orthodox, <b>Syriac</b>, Armenian and other communities, alongside Jews and Samaritans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The region's Christians lived through a general decline under Arabisation and Islamisation, yet <b>Syriac</b> Christianity passed through a “Syriac Renaissance” in which discriminatory rules were lifted, especially after the death of the conservative Nur al-Din in 1174, and illustrated Gospels were made at Mar Mattai near Mosul around…</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Early versions appeared in Latin, <b>Syriac</b> and Coptic; Jerome’s Latin Vulgate became the Bible of the medieval West; the missionaries Cyril and Methodius created a Slavonic script in the 9th century to translate the scriptures; and the Reformation produced vernacular Bibles such as Luther’s German Bible and the…</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Manichaean tradition names a canon of seven works written in <b>Syriac</b>, a dialect of Aramaic: the Living Gospel, the Treasure of Life, the Pragmateia, the Book of Mysteries, the Book of Giants, the Letters and the Psalms and Prayers.</blockquote><a href="/religion/manichaeism">Manichaeism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
